## Phase 14.1 — Structured ToolResult transport foundation

- Began Phase 14 using the revised Structured Tool Results and Workflow Output Contracts architecture decision.
- Added the shared `packages/tools` runtime foundation with:
  - versioned `ToolResult` envelope validation;
  - JSON-safe value, output type, message, nesting, and maximum-size guardrails;
  - wrapper-owned result paths under `logs/tool-results`;
  - atomic result writes and best-effort cleanup;
  - optional and required result-contract behavior;
  - explicit `legacy_tool_execution.v1` fallback results for non-migrated tools;
  - a reusable child-process adapter shared by API and worker tool execution.
- Updated API and worker tool launchers so every process receives the standard `SKYCOMMAND_TOOL_RESULT_*` environment contract while existing stdout/stderr capture and Tool History behavior remain intact.
- Added structured result contract status to execution metadata and exposed validated tool results on API/worker execution responses without storing the domain payload in audit metadata.
- Updated workflow tool nodes so stdout/stderr are no longer promoted into workflow output. Migrated tools use their emitted `ToolResult`; legacy tools receive an explicit completion object pointing diagnostics back to Tool History.
- Established canonical workflow context paths:
  - `nodes.<nodeKey>.result` for the complete validated envelope;
  - `nodes.<nodeKey>.output` for the domain payload;
  - matching `last.result` and `last.output` aliases.
- Updated condition-node lookup so custom domain fields can be read directly from `nodes.<nodeKey>.output.<customPath>` without knowing anything about the execution wrapper.
- Normalized node-output persistence to one authoritative `result` record and stores the declared `outputType` for structured tool results.
- Added `npm run tool-result:self-test` and included the shared contract/process adapter in `npm run validate`.
- Added `docs/SkyCommand_Phase_14_Structured_Tool_Results.md` and refreshed the README current status, architecture notes, design rules, documentation index, and roadmap. Phase 13 is now marked complete; Phase 14 is in progress; future registration, resilience, BI, and testing work has been placed into later roadmap phases.
- Added `.env.example` settings for result-size limits and gradual strict-tool migration through `SKYCOMMAND_TOOL_RESULT_REQUIRED_CODES`.
- No database migration is required for Phase 14.1. FRED, BoC, and StatCan remain legacy-result tools until their source-specific aggregation contracts are added in the next Phase 14 increments.

## Phase 14.2 — FRED structured macro-ingestion results

- Added `packages/ingestion/src/core/macroIngestionResult.js` with the reusable `macro_ingestion_summary.v1` contract, normalized indicator outcomes, aggregate totals, partial/failure semantics, and legacy CLI summary compatibility.
- Updated `copyLoader.js` so PostgreSQL copy/load statistics are parsed and returned as a structured object while the existing `staging_*`, `new_rows`, `inserted_rows`, and `target_max` console output remains intact.
- Added previous target maximum and staging minimum statistics so each indicator result can prove its before/source/after date coverage.
- Updated the shared ingestion pipeline and FRED batch runner to retain per-indicator row counts, date coverage, outcome, timing, and safe error details.
- Updated `loadFREDMacroData.js` to keep its existing CLI behavior while emitting a validated `macro_ingestion_summary.v1` ToolResult through the Phase 14 result channel when launched by SkyCommand.
- Made FRED the first strict structured-result migration in `.env.example` through `SKYCOMMAND_TOOL_RESULT_REQUIRED_CODES=ingestion_fred`.
- Updated Workflow History focused-node output so `macro_ingestion_summary.v1` uses a purpose-built run totals table and indicator result table instead of generic key/value flattening.
- Removed stdout/stderr preview parsing from the generic Workflow History output renderer; verbose script narration remains exclusively in Tool History.
- Added `npm run macro-ingestion:self-test` and included copy-stat parsing, aggregation, outcome semantics, and structured-result assertions in `npm run validate`.
- Updated README, Phase 14 documentation, and repository map for the Phase 14.2 proof case.
- No database migration or seed is required for this increment.

## Phase 14.2.1 — Structured-result dev watcher hotfix

- Diagnosed API `ECONNRESET` / Vite `Bad Gateway` responses and Temporal activity failures that occurred immediately after a migrated tool emitted its structured result. The wrapper-owned result file used a `.json` extension under `logs/tool-results`; development `nodemon` processes watch JSON files by default and restarted the API or Temporal worker as the result arrived.
- Added a repository `nodemon.json` that watches source folders only and explicitly ignores runtime logs, temporary ingestion folders, generated data, documentation, build output, and handoff archives.
- Updated all development nodemon scripts to load the shared configuration explicitly.
- Changed ephemeral structured-result transport files from `.json` to the non-source `.tool-result` extension as defense in depth. The payload remains validated JSON; only the temporary transport filename changed.
- Extended the ToolResult self-test to verify the opaque transport extension. Wrapper cleanup continues deleting the result and temporary files after reading, so an empty `logs/tool-results` directory after execution is expected.


## Phase 14.2.2 — Semantic output persistence and retry-boundary fix

- Diagnosed workflow-only FRED failures after successful tool execution. `worker.workflow_run_node_outputs.output_type` still enforced the original Phase 13 structural JSON whitelist, while Phase 14 correctly persisted the semantic ToolResult contract name `macro_ingestion_summary.v1`; PostgreSQL rejected the node-output record during completion persistence.
- Added migration `00062__workflow_output_type_contracts.sql` to replace the structural whitelist with a non-blank semantic contract check, and updated migration `00055` so fresh databases use the corrected definition.
- Preserved backward compatibility: generic node outputs may still store `object`, `array`, `string`, and other structural labels, while migrated tools store versioned contracts.
- Moved the Temporal completed-node log message after successful ledger persistence so terminal output no longer claims completion before persistence finishes.
- Separated tool execution retries from completion-ledger retries. A successful side-effecting tool is no longer executed again when only output/context persistence fails; the idempotent completion activity uses its own Temporal retry policy and surfaces a dedicated persistence failure if exhausted.
- Updated README, Phase 14 documentation, and repository map.
- Database migration `00062` is required before retesting the FRED workflow.

## Phase 14.3 — BoC and StatCan structured results

- Added `packages/ingestion/src/core/macroIngestionCli.js`, a reusable source-neutral CLI adapter that invokes the domain runner, preserves the existing console presentation, creates `macro_ingestion_summary.v1`, emits through the wrapper-owned result channel, converts fatal execution errors into structured results, and preserves `--allow-failures` exit semantics.
- Refactored FRED to use the shared macro-ingestion CLI adapter without changing its specialized batch runner or familiar console summary.
- Migrated Bank of Canada and Statistics Canada ingestion entrypoints to emit the same normalized `macro_ingestion_summary.v1` contract used by FRED. No source-specific workflow wrappers were introduced.
- Exported source execution and main functions so the CLI adapters can be tested and later consumed by manifest/contract-check infrastructure without duplicating domain logic.
- Made `ingestion_fred`, `ingestion_boc`, and `ingestion_statcan` strict structured-result tools in `.env.example`.
- Added `npm run macro-ingestion-cli:self-test` and included shared-adapter success, fatal failure, partial-result, allow-failures, source-code, and emission checks in `npm run validate`.
- Updated README, Phase 14 documentation, roadmap wording, and repository map.
- No database migration or seed is required for Phase 14.3.


## Phase 14.4 — Tool manifest and contract-check foundation

- Added versioned `skycommand.tool.json` manifests for FRED, Bank of Canada, and Statistics Canada. Each manifest declares repository-relative entrypoint, parameters and bindings, permission, execution policy, semantic result contract, output schema, and non-destructive sample result.
- Added a dependency-free JSON Schema validator supporting the object, array, scalar, enum, local-reference, composition, bounds, pattern, and date/date-time rules used by SkyCommand contracts.
- Added strict `skycommand.tool.v1` manifest validation with path traversal/symlink protection, supported runtime and binding checks, unique parameter/permission enforcement, secret-binding safeguards, default-value checks, referenced-file validation, and SHA-256 hashes for manifests, entrypoints, output schemas, and samples.
- Added the strict `macro_ingestion_summary.v1` output JSON Schema and updated `validateToolResult()` so a result can be checked against both its declared semantic output type and its domain schema.
- Added the generic `runToolCli()` adapter with non-destructive `--skycommand-describe` and `--skycommand-contract-check` modes. The macro ingestion adapter now delegates normal execution and special contract modes to this shared SDK layer.
- Added repository-wide manifest discovery, validation, description, and contract-check commands:
  - `npm run tool-manifest:discover`
  - `npm run tool-manifest:validate`
  - `npm run tool-contract:check`
  - `npm run tool-manifest:self-test`
- Added a runtime manifest registry. API and worker tool execution now resolve manifests generically, enforce manifest-required results, apply manifest timeouts, validate the emitted output again after process completion, and reject script-path/runtime/permission drift before launch.
- Added manifest and contract-check self-tests covering valid discovery, strict output schemas, unsafe paths, secret command-line bindings, describe/contract-check non-execution guarantees, registry contracts, and drift detection.
- Updated README, Phase 14 documentation, roadmap wording, and repository map.
- No database migration or seed is required for Phase 14.4; hash persistence and transactional registration remain later increments.

## Phase 14.5 — Structured workflow consumption and summary aggregation

- Added `packages/tools/src/workflowResultContext.js`, a deterministic shared helper used by both inline API execution and the Temporal workflow bundle to expose the complete ToolResult under `nodes.<nodeKey>.result` and the domain payload under `nodes.<nodeKey>.output`.
- Aligned `warnings`, `error`, `metadata`, `last.result`, `last.output`, `previousResult`, and `previousOutput` aliases across inline and Temporal-backed workflows.
- Fixed the Temporal in-memory result scope so condition nodes and runtime templates no longer see the wrapper envelope where the documented domain output path is expected.
- Added explicit `WORKFLOW_CONDITION_PATH_NOT_FOUND` failures when a configured condition path is missing and no fallback value is supplied; condition results now record whether the path resolved or used a fallback.
- Reworked Summary-node key outputs into compact, bounded result indexes rather than JSON output previews.
- Added normalized macro-ingestion rollups containing every FRED, Bank of Canada, and Statistics Canada source plus combined requested/succeeded/updated/unchanged/failed/staged/new/inserted totals.
- Added a purpose-built Workflow History Summary-node renderer with symmetric macro-source rows, a combined totals row, and a concise node-result index.
- Added compact ToolResult evidence to scheduled tool-run metadata without copying indicator arrays, stdout, or stderr into the scheduler ledger.
- Added `npm run workflow-result-context:self-test` and included canonical path, three-source aggregation, compact summary, and scheduled-result assertions in `npm run validate`.
- Updated condition and summary editor guidance, README, Phase 14 documentation, and repository map.
- No database migration or seed is required for Phase 14.5.

## Phase 14.6 — Accepted manifest snapshots and runtime drift enforcement

- Added migration `00063__tool_manifest_registration_snapshots.sql` with versioned historical snapshots for accepted `skycommand.tool.json` contracts, including source repository, manifest path/version, runtime, entrypoint, semantic output type, required-result flag, normalized manifest JSON, validator version, validation status, and SHA-256 hashes for the manifest, entrypoint, output schema, and contract sample.
- Added a partial unique current-snapshot constraint, historical `SUPERSEDED` retention, status/hash indexes, updated-at maintenance, and `core.vw_tool_manifest_snapshot_status`.
- Added reusable manifest snapshot preview, sync, check, candidate, and drift-comparison services under `packages/tools`.
- Added CLI commands:
  - `npm run tool-manifest:snapshot:preview`
  - `npm run tool-manifest:snapshot:sync`
  - `npm run tool-manifest:snapshot:check`
- Snapshot sync accepts validated repository changes while blocking database registry mismatches or unregistered manifest tools. Prior accepted snapshots remain historical rather than being overwritten.
- API and worker tool queries now load the current accepted snapshot. Manifest-backed tools fail before launch when no accepted snapshot exists or when repository files/contract metadata differ from the current `VALID` snapshot.
- Added `TOOL_MANIFEST_SNAPSHOT_REQUIRED` and `TOOL_MANIFEST_SNAPSHOT_DRIFT` enforcement to the generic manifest registry contract.
- Added accepted snapshot ID/status/hash evidence to script execution metadata and returned execution details.
- Added Tool Manifest Registration checks and snapshot commands to Production Readiness.
- Added `npm run tool-manifest-snapshot:self-test` and included snapshot candidate, matching, drift, missing, unregistered, and registry-mismatch scenarios in `npm run validate`.
- Updated README, Phase 14 documentation, roadmap wording, and repository map.
- Migration `00063` and an initial `npm run tool-manifest:snapshot:sync` are required before running manifest-backed tools after this increment.


## Phase 14.7 — Repository package structured-result proof

- Migrated the existing `repo_zip_generate` file tool to the same generic `runToolCli()` adapter used by manifest-backed ingestion tools, without adding a repository-specific workflow wrapper.
- Preserved direct CLI and Run Tools console output while emitting the strict `repository_package_summary.v1` ToolResult for workflows and schedules.
- Added repository package output fields for archive path, repository name/root, file count, source/archive bytes, compression ratio, duration, and packaging policy.
- Added a versioned `skycommand.tool.json` manifest, strict output JSON Schema, non-destructive contract-check sample, and accepted-snapshot participation for `repo_zip_generate`.
- Added purpose-built Workflow History Artifact Summary and Packaging Policy tables for focused repository-package nodes.
- Added compact repository-package evidence to scheduled result metadata and verified canonical condition paths such as `nodes.<nodeKey>.output.filesIncluded` and `nodes.<nodeKey>.output.artifactPath`.
- Added `npm run repository-package:self-test` and included repository archive generation, sensitive-file exclusion, result validation, condition lookup, summary compaction, and scheduled metadata checks in `npm run validate`.
- No database migration or seed is required. Because this increment adds a new manifest-backed tool, run snapshot preview, sync, and check after merging.

## Phase 14.8.1 — Manifest sample recovery and per-tool failure isolation

- Restored the repository-map and Dev Commit non-destructive contract-check samples required by their `skycommand.tool.json` manifests.
- Diagnosed `resultContract.samplePath was not found` as a missing repository validation artifact, not a `SKYCOMMAND_TOOL_RESULT_REQUIRED_CODES` failure. The environment override is evaluated after manifest resolution and was not responsible for the outage.
- Changed runtime manifest lookup from eager validation of every repository manifest to isolated lookup and validation of the requested tool only.
- A broken or incomplete manifest now blocks its own tool while unrelated accepted tools remain executable. Repository-wide `npm run validate`, manifest validation, snapshot preview/sync/check, and readiness checks remain deliberately strict and still report every broken manifest.
- Added a regression test proving that a tool with a valid contract can load when an unrelated manifest references a missing sample, while the broken tool still fails with `TOOL_MANIFEST_REFERENCED_FILE_MISSING`.
- No database migration or seed is required. Re-run validation and the manifest snapshot lifecycle after restoring the files.

## 2026-07-17 - Phase 14 workflow execution and history control hardening
- Authorized a high-risk workflow tool only when the immediately preceding result is an approved human checkpoint; the configured confirmation phrase is passed through the generic workflow tool bridge.
- Kept failed/terminated workflow focus on the terminal issue node while preserving unrestricted manual inspection of every graph node, including completed and not-run nodes.
- Manual node selection now disables Follow active node so polling cannot immediately override the operator's choice.
- Replaced runtime graph `scrollIntoView` with horizontal viewport scrolling so Workflow History polling and row selection never pull the page down from the run table.

## 2026-07-18 - Phase 14.15 closure and documentation

- Marked Phase 14 complete after the eight-node Development Promotion workflow successfully consumed `git_repository_status.v1`, evaluated `nodes.repo_intel_node.output.readyForDevelopmentPromotion`, followed the true branch, completed approval and branch synchronization, and produced the final structured promotion summary.
- Updated README status and roadmap language so Phase 15 Tool Catalogue administration is the next planned increment.
- Finalized the Phase 14 architecture reference around the fail-open execution rule: the existing PostgreSQL tool catalogue remains the runtime authority, while structured-result transport, schema validation, renderers, and scheduled evidence remain supplementary reporting concerns after successful domain execution.
- Removed the remaining obsolete repository-contract planning language from the Word architecture plan and replaced it with the delivered PostgreSQL-catalogue onboarding handoff for Phase 15.
- Preserved strict condition-path behavior, purpose-built Workflow History rendering, Tool History log authority, wrapper-owned result transport, and recovery access for Repository Map, Repository ZIP, and Dev Commit.
- No database migration, seed, API, worker, Temporal, or Admin-Web code change is required for Phase 14.15.

## 2026-07-21 - Phase 15.6 PostgreSQL comparison proof package and database-health structured output

- Prepared the first real Phase 15 onboarding proof package, `db_object_compare`, as a separate upload-ready bundle rather than pre-installing it under `packages` and causing a managed-destination collision.
- The tool accepts two PostgreSQL database names, loads migration-relevant catalogue objects from both databases, compares object presence and stable definition fingerprints in both directions, prints human-readable differences, and emits `postgresql_database_comparison_summary.v1` with `databasesMatch`, counts, per-type totals, and bounded difference details.
- A completed comparison remains a successful tool execution even when the databases differ; workflows branch on `nodes.<nodeKey>.output.databasesMatch`. Connection, authentication, or catalogue-query errors remain genuine failures.
- Upgraded `packages/db/src/db_health.js` to check one or two named databases, emit `database_health_summary.v1`, expose `allOnline`, retain direct-CLI strict failure by default, and allow catalogue/workflow calls to pass `failWhenOffline=false` so a condition node can decide whether to continue.
- Added `00069__db_health_structured_output_seed.sql`, the database-health output schema, recovery-manifest parameters, and a structured-result self-test.
- Refined Add Tool static analysis so flexible destinations can use any correct relative CommonJS import resolving to `packages/tools/src`; the analyzer no longer assumes only the default `../../src` package depth.
- Refined the Tool Authoring Guide and AI build prompt with real-package lessons: `require.main` testability, shared success/failure schema shape, negative domain outcomes versus execution failures, isolated PostgreSQL pools, safe fingerprint output, bounded details, and workflow condition guidance.
- Word architecture-plan updates remain paused only because of the current DOCX viewer/download issue; Markdown documentation continues normally.

## 2026-07-21 - Phase 15.6 descriptor lifecycle and entrypoint normalization

- Fixed Add Tool static analysis so a descriptor may declare the canonical managed entrypoint `tool.js` even when the administrator uploads a descriptively named source file such as `db_object_compare.js`.
- The analyzer now accepts either the uploaded filename or `tool.js`, reports the managed-name normalization as informational evidence, and blocks only unrelated descriptor entrypoints.
- Clarified that `skycommand.tool.json` is a per-tool onboarding descriptor, not a repository-wide registry: the `_template` descriptor remains untouched, every tool receives its own descriptor in its own package directory, and registration regenerates the canonical descriptor from administrator-approved configuration.
- Reaffirmed that PostgreSQL remains runtime authority; descriptors, hashes, and preview evidence are never execution gates.
- Added regression coverage for custom upload filenames with canonical managed entrypoints and updated the Tool Authoring Guide, AI build prompt, and Phase 15 Markdown plan.
- No database migration or seed is required.
